A||||12||fogglethorpe||Man on the Sidewalk||||9||
Z||000000||fogglethorpe||09-29-2001||05:57 PM||||the sun comes up the rush begins<BR>in cool or hot in rain or wind<BR>briefcases in hand they step around<BR>a figure lying on the ground<BR>with work to do and bills to pay<BR>there is no time for mercy today<BR>for the man on the sidewalk<P>chasing after their own desires<BR>building personal empires<BR>with frenzied haste in every direction<BR>too much too busy to pay attention<BR>to the man on the sidewalk<P>no one approaches no one will dare<BR>entertain an angel unaware<BR>on parkbenches they feed the birds<BR>but never utter a single word<BR>to the man on the sidewalk<P>chasing after their own desires<BR>building personal empires<BR>with frenzied haste in every direction<BR>too much too busy to pay attention<BR>to the man on the sidewalk<P>the sun goes down the day is done<BR>everyone's gone except for one<BR>but surely they'll return tomorrow<BR>to once again ignore the sorrow<BR>of the man on the sidewalk<P>chasing after their own desires<BR>building personal empires<BR>with frenzied haste in every direction<BR>too much too busy to pay attention<BR>everyone takes everyone keeps<BR>but no one prays no one weeps<BR>for the man...<BR>||||reg||9
Z||000001||maryjane||09-29-2001||06:45 PM||||Noble, thought provoking and inspiring words, especially in these troubled times. I've just seen too many able bodied men standing on the side of the road with the "need work, family to feed, disabled vet"<BR>signs, to get too upset anymore. I've offered some of em jobs, they refuse. This is a true story that happened to me, kinda long & a little funny:<BR>I'm fishing one day here in San Angelo in a small river, not much bigger than a stream, but pretty deep. It starts raining, so I move under a bridge  nearby, and continue fishing. Looking up under the bridge across the river, I see a homeless guy laying down, I see he is watching me. It's hot as hell & I wonder how he's  making it. I think about the $ I've spent on bait ,ice & beer for the fishing & feel a little guilty. I reel in my line, remove the minnow, fold up a $5 bill, and hook it on. I cast it up to the other side. He doesn't move much. I yell over that there is a 5 dollar bill on the hook. He looks down toward it, and says,"can you come over the bridge & give it to me? It's too hot to get up & come down there." It's only about 15 feet to the hook & line, & pouring down rain too much to go out & cross the bridge. I think the hell with this lazy ass, & just jerk the rod back, to avoid dragging the bill thru the water. The hook,$5, & sinker come flying back & hit me squarely in the forehead & face. He's laughin & I didn't feel guilty anymore. He just lays back down & goes back to sleep.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cheever3000||09-29-2001||06:55 PM||||Oh! I thought it was talking about one of those chalk outlines drawn where a body was found.  Silly me.<P>But regarding panhandlers with the signs, I have seen that they were sometimes followed at the end of the day to see where they went, and turned out they owned nice houses with nice cars in the garage!  I don't give anything to those people.  I have a heart, and I give charitably, but it's to my church.  They are able to do the most good with it, including for the homeless, disabled, retarded, and locked up.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||DaRkLoRD||09-29-2001||07:00 PM||||I once heard of a guy who was begging for money outside of a bank in Toronto.. turns out he was pulling in $300/day, and had a house outside of the city, a nice car, etc...<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||4
Z||000004||maryjane||09-29-2001||07:00 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cheever3000:<BR><B>Oh! I thought it was talking about one of those chalk outlines drawn where a body was found.  Silly me.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Hmmm, maybe he was. Never thought about it. We don't have many sidewalks. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pontiac_5.jpg"> <BR><p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 09-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||fogglethorpe||09-29-2001||07:31 PM||||Just to clarify...this is not a rant against capitalism, or a homeless advocacy tome. I'm not into that stuff. It is simply an observation about human nature.<BR>Also, no one should EVER feel guilty for being prosperous. Grateful, yes, but not guilty.||||reg||1
